    "_postman_id": "4459891e-76a2-4800-a16a-9593ec6664d6",
    "name": "e-commerce API · users",
    "description": "The user endpoints of the e-commerce learning repo, served by `UserController` at the `users` root.\n\n**Before running:** start the API with `pnpm start:dev`, which brings up Postgres, Mongo and Mailpit through Docker Compose and boots Nest on the port in `.env` (3000 by default).\n\n**Variables:** `baseUrl` and the account credentials come from the shared **e-commerce (local)** environment; select it before running. Only `userId` stays a collection variable, because nothing outside this collection reads it.\n\n**Auth model:** `JwtAuthGuard` is registered as `APP_GUARD`, so every endpoint here except the public `POST /users` needs a bearer access token. The collection sets bearer `{{accessToken}}` at the collection level; run Login in the auth-context collection to populate it.\n\n**Run order:** `Create user` stores the new id in the `userId` collection variable, and every later request reads it. Run it first, or set `userId` by hand.\n\n**What the domain normalises**, so a value comes back different from what you sent: the email is trimmed and lowercased in full, so `Bob@Example.com` and `bob@example.com` are one identity and the unique constraint compares case-insensitively; the phone has spaces, dots, hyphens and parentheses stripped and is stored as a leading `+` followed by 8 to 15 digits; the role is trimmed and lowercased, so `Seller` is accepted and stored as `seller`.\n\n**Status codes:** 400 comes from the validation pipe and carries no `code` field, while 422, 404 and 409 come from the typed exception filters and carry a stable `code`. Validity is decided before existence, so a request that is both invalid and aimed at a missing id answers 422, not 404.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
